---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { bank } from '../data/bank';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH, MAX_REPLY_TOKENS, MESSAGES_PER_LEVEL, MESSAGES_PER_RUN, RUNS_PER_DAY, RUNS_PER_VISITOR } from '../engine/budget';
import { CLUE_COST } from '../engine/score';
import { TEXT_DAYS } from '../engine/retention';
import { levels } from '../levels';

// How the game is built, what it defends against, what it logs and what it can't do.
// The source for the portfolio case study. Every number comes from the code it describes.
const parts = [
  {
    title: 'The stack',
    body: [
      `Astro on one Cloudflare Worker. The pages are built ahead of time, and the API runs on the Worker next to them. D1 holds players, progress and the message log. Workers AI runs the bank's chatbots: Llama 3.1 8B, and the smaller Llama 3.2 1B on level 2, where a keyword filter does the defending.`,
      'Replies stream to the page as server-sent events, through plain TypeScript with no UI framework. The whole thing runs on the free plan with no card on the account, so going over a limit stops the game for the day and never sends a bill.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Why the prompts are public',
    body: [
      `The code is public, prompts and defences included. A defence that only works while nobody has read the prompt is no defence at all, and the debriefs say so level after level.`,
      'What stays private is the key on the server. Every secret is worked out from it per player and per level, so the answer you find is yours alone and never sits in a file or the database.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Keeping it fair',
    body: [
      'The server decides everything that counts. It builds the prompt, applies the filters, checks for the leak, keeps the score and reads the chat history from its own log, so editing the page or replaying a request changes nothing.',
      `Each level allows ${MESSAGES_PER_LEVEL} messages and three hints. A level whose clue you have seen is worth ${CLUE_COST} less, recorded before the clue is sent.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Keeping it running',
    body: [
      `The AI allowance is shared, so a model call needs a reserved run first. There are ${RUNS_PER_DAY} runs of ${MESSAGES_PER_RUN} messages a day, and one connection can start ${RUNS_PER_VISITOR} of them. Messages are capped at ${MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH} characters and replies at ${MAX_REPLY_TOKENS} tokens.`,
      "A message the filter blocks never reaches the model, so it costs the allowance nothing. If the allowance runs out anyway, the message is handed back.",
    ],
  },
  {
    title: "What's logged",
    body: [
      `What you type and what the bot replies, tied to a random ID in a cookie. No name, email or password. The text is wiped after ${TEXT_DAYS} days by a daily job, and only the counts behind scores stay.`,
      'Your IP address is never stored. The daily run limit counts a scrambled version that changes every day and is deleted the next. A shared report shows scores, never what you typed.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Honest limits',
    body: [
      'Small models are inconsistent. The same message can win once and miss the next time, and each level was tuned by playtesting, not proven.',
      `The run limit is per connection, so someone with many addresses can still fill the day. The worst case is a full day, never a bill. ${levels.length} of 6 levels are live: the banking agent and the boss come next.`,
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base title="How it's built · Break the AI" description="How Break the AI is built: the stack, the defences, what's logged and what it can't do.">
  <section class="head wrap" aria-labelledby="about-title">
    <h1 id="about-title">How it's built</h1>
    <p class="lead">A game about attacking AI has to expect attacks. This is the stack, the defences, what gets logged and what it still can't do.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="parts wrap" aria-label="About the game">
    {parts.map((part) => (
      <div class="part">
        <h2>{part.title}</h2>
        <div class="part-body">
          {part.body.map((p) => <p>{p}</p>)}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="on wrap" aria-labelledby="on-title">
    <hr class="dots" />
    <h2 id="on-title" class="visually-hidden">Next</h2>
    <p class="on-note">Made by {site.name}. {bank.name} and everyone in it are made up.</p>
    <div class="on-row">
      <a class="pill pill--big" href="/">Play</a>
      <a class="pill pill--quiet" href={site.repo}>Read the code</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: clamp(24px, 6vh, 72px);
  }

  .head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-head);
    line-height: 0.95;
  }

  .lead {
    margin-top: 20px;
    max-width: 44ch;
    font-size: var(--step-lead);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .parts {
    padding-top: 48px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .part {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
    gap: 24px;
    padding: clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .part h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-lead);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }

  .part-body {
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .on {
    padding-block: 56px 48px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
  }

  .on-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .on-note {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .part {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 12px;
    }

    .on-row .pill {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
